The game map should be substantially larger than the visible mobile screen. The camera must follow the player while only displaying a small section of the overall world.

Divide the world into connected areas:

1. Radhanagar Main Village.
2. Traditional Bengali residential neighborhoods.
3. Large green paddy fields.
4. Village market and shopping area.
5. Riverbank and ferry terminal.
6. Rural highways and dirt roads.
7. Fishing village.
8. School and playground.
9. Village health center.
10. Mosque and open community grounds.
11. Agricultural farms.
12. Mango, coconut, banana, and jackfruit gardens.
13. Railway crossing or rural transport junction.
14. Bus stand and vehicle garage.
15. Riverside settlement.
16. Remote countryside and hidden exploration areas.

Connect these areas using dirt roads, paved rural roads, narrow paths, small bridges, and winding village lanes.

The map should include multiple routes, shortcuts, bridges, field boundaries, ponds, canals, and areas that can be discovered through exploration.

Create a coherent world layout. Roads must connect correctly, houses must sit on suitable terrain, rivers must not block essential routes, and buildings must not randomly overlap.

The world should feel handcrafted and geographically consistent.

If the entire world cannot fit in memory, use chunk-based world loading or another efficient world-streaming approach. Load nearby scenery and NPCs while unloading distant nonessential objects.

The player must not see the world ending abruptly at the edge of the mobile screen.

