22. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

Choose an appropriate 2D game framework that can be implemented and run reliably in the available Google AI Studio development environment.

Prefer a reliable HTML5 Canvas-based engine or a suitable established 2D game framework, such as Phaser, if the environment supports it.

Use a modular architecture separating:

- Main game initialization.
- World and terrain.
- Camera.
- Player movement.
- NPC behavior.
- Vehicle physics.
- Collision detection.
- Missions.
- Phone interface.
- Minimap and world map.
- Audio manager.
- UI and menus.
- Save system.
- Settings.
- Asset loading.

Use a fixed or carefully controlled update loop, delta-time-aware movement, efficient rendering, and proper cleanup of input and audio resources.

Use a tilemap or another scalable representation for the large world.

Keep gameplay logic separate from UI rendering wherever practical.

Use reusable systems instead of duplicating logic across every NPC, vehicle, and mission.

Do not depend on Firebase or a backend unless a genuinely required online feature is added later. The initial game must be playable offline after its required assets have loaded, wherever browser limitations permit.

23. PERFORMANCE AND ANDROID OPTIMIZATION

Target smooth performance on ordinary Android phones.

Use:

- Efficient sprite rendering.
- Camera-based culling.
- Limited active NPC simulation outside the visible area.
- Reusable objects where appropriate.
- Compressed and appropriately sized assets.
- Optimized collision checks.
- Lazy loading for distant world sections.
- Controlled particle counts.
- Lightweight UI updates.
- Efficient audio playback.
- Touch input designed for mobile browsers.

Avoid excessive memory use, unnecessary animation loops, unbounded object creation, and huge assets that slow down loading.

Provide low, medium, and high visual-quality modes where practical.

Prevent accidental page reloads, horizontal page scrolling, and loss of input focus during gameplay.

24. GAME WORLD CONTENT AND VARIETY

Make the world feel different throughout the day.

Markets should become busier during appropriate hours. Farmers should appear in fields. School areas should show activity at suitable times. Some shops may close at night. Roads should contain occasional moving vehicles.

Include decorative posters and Bengali signboards throughout the village.

Use fictional local shop names, road signs, school names, and village landmarks. Avoid using real people's private information.

Give different areas unique scenery and ambient audio so that the world does not feel repetitive.

Add hidden paths, scenic riverbanks, quiet rural courtyards, and distant farmland to encourage exploration.

